ePiX -- INSTALL

Last Change: January 05, 2005

This file contains detailed installation instructions for ePiX-1.x

Lines starting with a "%" denote commands typed at a shell prompt. 
Lines starting with a "#" denote commands run as root.
Don't type the prompt characters "%" or "#". :)


The following software must be installed on your system in order for
you to use ePiX:

  * The GNU shell, bash
  * A C++ compiler and development libraries, preferably g++
  * GhostScript, ImageMagick, and teTeX
  * GNU binutils, textutils, and fileutils (ar, sed, grep, ln, rm...)

(For the curious, the specific programs are: convert, dvips, g++,
latex, and ps2epsi.) Please consult your system documentation for
information on installing these components. The POST-INSTALL file has
potentially helpful information, as well.

ePiX comprises a header file (epix.h), a compiled library (libepix.a),
and four shell scripts (epix, elaps, flix, and laps) that automate
file processing by running auxiliary programs.  The shell scripts must
be in your PATH.


--- Synopsis ---

(1) Unpack
(2) Edit the Makefile if installing somewhere other than /usr/local
(3) Edit and run newbash.sh if bash is not /bin/bash
(4) Build:

% make [contrib] test 
% make install


--- Step-by-step Instructions ---

(1) Tarballs are available gzip or bzip2 compressed. GNU/Linux comes with
  bzip2 utilities, while some Unices may not. To unpack the tarball, use
  the appropriate version number, package name (src or complete), and
  tar options (z for .gz, j for .bz2, v to see filenames unpacked):

% tar -zxf epix-x.y.z_complete.tar.gz

% tar -jxf epix-x.y.z_complete.tar.bz2

  If your "tar" doesn't understand decompression, do

% gunzip -c epix-x.y.z_complete.tar.gz | tar -xf -

% bzcat epix-x.y.z_complete.tar.bz2 | tar -xf -

  Change to the source directory:

% cd epix-x.y.z


(2) To install in /usr/local, skip to step (3). Otherwise, edit the
  following line of the config file as desired:

  EPIX_ROOTDIR=/usr/local


(3) As already noted, ePiX is strongly bash-dependent.

  If bash is in /bin/bash on your system, skip to step (4).

  If bash is NOT in /bin/bash, then edit the file newbash.sh, replacing
  "/usr/local/bin/bash" in the indicated line with the path to bash on
  your system, then run the script:

% ./newbash.sh

  When the script finishes, you may safely remove the ".orig" files left 
  behind. If the script fails, something is seriously wrong, e.g., you
  don't have write permission in the source directory, or "sed" is not
  installed. Speak to your sysadmin for assistance. The action of the 
  script can be accomplished manually (but painfully) as follows: For
  each file listed in the script, replace each occurrence of "/bin/bash"
  with the full path to bash on your system (about 10 changes in all).


(4) Compile:

% make

  This builds several object files, and the following files for
  installation:
  * A static library (libepix.a)
  * A header file (epix.h)
  * The shell scripts

  If desired, build the (separately documented, see the contrib/ directory)
  contributed package:

% make contrib

% make test

  Builds the documentation and sample files. These may be built/cleaned 
  individually ("make doc", "make samples-clean", etc.)


(5) Install:

  You must have write permission in the install directory you selected
  in step (2). If you have been logged on as yourself until now (a Very
  Good Idea:) and are installing in a system directory, do:

% su
Password: <your root password>

# make install

  (The root prompt on many systems is # rather than %.) If the install
  succeeds, you're done:

# exit

  If you are installing in your HOME directory, just type

% make install


(6) Post-Install:

  From the source directory, do
  
% make clean

  to clean the source tree of compiled files, documentation, and samples.
  It is safe to run "make clean" repeatedly.

  The documentation and sample files are installed in $INSTALL_DIR/doc,
  as compiled files (ps, pdf) and tarred LaTeX sources.

  The directory $INSTALL_DIR/share/epix/config contains miscellaneous
  optional files, notably Jay Belanger's emacs mode for working with
  ePiX source files, and completions for users of Ian MacDonald's bash
  completions package. POST-INSTALL has instructions on installing and
  using these files.


(7) Uninstalling:

  To remove ePiX from your system, go to the directory where the 
  sources were unpacked, "su root" if necessary, and do:

% make -n uninstall-all
       ^^ N.B.!

  FAILURE TO PERFORM THIS SANITY CHECK MAY DAMAGE YOUR FILESYSTEM!!

  If the install path (in make's output) is correct, it is safe to do

% make uninstall-all

  ePiX also provides a "make uninstall" target, which removes only the
  version corresponding to the source directory. 


--- Installing Multiple Versions ---

More than one version of ePiX can be installed on the same machine. To
install a version older than 1.0.3, download and build the source code
as usual -- please visit

  http://savannah.nongnu.org/cgi-bin/viewcvs/epix/epix/archive/

for older versions -- then manually install the header and library, e.g.

# install -m 644 epix.h /usr/local/include/epix-0.6.5.h
# install -m 644 libepix.a /usr/local/lib/libepix-0.6.5.a

Note that the version number must be specified in the target names, and
that old shell scripts must NOT be installed. To use version 0.6.5 (say),
the source file must #include "epix-0.6.5.h" and be compiled with the
epix flag "--no-defaults", e.g.

% epix --no-defaults -I/usr/local/include -L/usr/local/lib -lepix-0.6.5 file.c



